export const PP1={title:'The event, the team and the score',hook:'Parliamentary Procedure turns a group of five to eight HOSA members into a model business meeting. After a 50-question test on Robert’s Rules, top teams get a secret topic, 15 minutes to plan and 10 minutes to run a complete meeting in front of judges—processing a main motion and four required motions with the right words, in the right order, with every member involved.',objectives:['Describe the event’s rounds, materials and timing.','Explain the Round One test plan and references.','Assign meeting roles across a five- to eight-member team.','Identify how the 162-point meeting rubric is organized.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Parliamentary Procedure is a **team event for 5–8 members** in the **secondary** and **postsecondary/collegiate** divisions, each with an identified office or role (president, secretary, treasurer, committee chair, members). **Round One** is a **50-item multiple-choice test in 60 minutes**; the team’s **average** qualifies teams for Round Two and is **added** to the meeting score for final results. There are no verbal time announcements in the Testing Center.',
'In **Round Two**, teams receive a **secret topic/agenda** with **one main motion and four subsidiary and/or privileged motions** that must be included. Teams have **15 minutes to plan** (with time warnings at 5 and 1 minute), then **10 minutes** to conduct the meeting, timed from the opening gavel to the closing gavel, with a card at one minute remaining.',
'New for 2026–27, the meeting presentation is **10 minutes**. In a tie, the higher test average decides. NAP offers recognition to members scoring 70% or higher on the test.',
{t:'table',caption:'Event structure',head:['Part','Time','Notes'],rows:[['Round One test','60 minutes','50 items; team average counts'],['Meeting preparation','15 minutes','Secret topic; RONR allowed'],['Meeting presentation','10 minutes','Gavel to gavel; 162-point rubric']]}]],
['Materials and references',[
'Teams prepare in advance and bring **minutes of a previous chapter meeting**, a **treasurer’s report** and **committee report(s)**. Each member receives a copy of the secret topic and may write on it. Teams may also bring **blank paper** and a **pencil for the president**.',
'During **preparation only**, teams may use **Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised In Brief** or **Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised** (HOSA provides one copy of RONR). Neither may be used during the meeting itself. The secret topic is **confidential**; discussing it before the event ends violates professional ethics and is penalized.',
'Test references are RONR, RONR In Brief and the **NAP Study Questions for the Membership Exam**; the **test plan is 100% National Association of Parliamentarians** content.',
sort('Allowed in the meeting room?',['Allowed','Not allowed'],[['Minutes of the previous meeting',0],['Robert’s Rules book',1],['Treasurer’s report',0],['Secret topic copies with prep notes',0],['A phone with notes',1],['Blank paper and the president’s pencil',0]])]],
['The Round One test plan',[
'Test topics follow RONR In Brief: **why have rules**, **what happens at a meeting**, **handling motions**, **debate**, **amendments**, **postponing and referring to a committee**, **how a group can change its mind**, **voting and elections**, **bylaws and other rules**, **how rules are enforced and suspended**, the **table of rules** and **officers, committee chairmen and members**.',
'Sample items ask how special committee members are chosen (if the bylaws are silent, the method is included in the motion to commit), when a two-thirds vote is required (to close debate or extend limits of debate) and the highest level of rules in an organization’s documents.',
'Every member takes the test, so every member must study the whole book.',
match('Test topic and example question',[['Debate','How many times may a member speak?'],['Amendments','What is a secondary amendment?'],['Changing its mind','Who may move to reconsider?'],['Voting','What does a majority vote mean?'],['Bylaws','Which rules rank highest?'],['Enforcing rules','How does a point of order work?']])]],
['Team roles',[
'Every member must take an **active role**. Typical roles: the **president** (presiding officer) runs the meeting; the **secretary** keeps and reads or references minutes; the **treasurer** gives the treasurer’s report; **committee chairs** give committee reports; and **members** make motions, second, debate and use incidental motions. Larger teams can include a vice president or more committee chairs.',
'The **presiding officer’s skill and knowledge** is scored separately, so choose a confident, well-prepared president. But team participation is also scored—every member should speak meaningfully.',
"Because roles are drawn from your own team, practice with members rotating roles. If someone is ill on competition day, another member can step into the presidency or treasurer role without losing points.",
flip('Meeting roles',[['President','Presides and processes motions'],['Secretary','Minutes of the previous meeting'],['Treasurer','Treasurer’s report'],['Committee chair','Committee report and recommendations'],['Members','Make motions, second and debate'],['Timekeeper (judges)','Shows one-minute card']])]],
['How the meeting is scored',[
'The **162-point** rubric has five sections. **Proper order of business** (3 points each, yes or no): call to order, approval of minutes, treasurer’s report, committee report(s), unfinished business, new business (secret topic) and adjournment. **Motions** (maximum 56): the main motion, the four secret-topic motions and up to two optional additional motions, each judged on correct demonstration.',
'**General parliamentary procedure** (10 each): proper recognition of chair and members, proper parliamentary terms for the four required motions, the agenda and the skill and knowledge of the presiding officer. **Delivery**: voice, stage presence and diction (5 each), plus team participation and quality of discussion (10 each). **Minutes** of the previous meeting (10).',
'Minutes do not have to be read aloud; it is acceptable to say they were previously distributed.',
match('Rubric section and example item',[['Order of business','Treasurer’s report presented'],['Motions','Secret-topic motion demonstrated correctly'],['General procedure','Members recognized properly'],['Delivery','Quality of discussion'],['Minutes','Previous minutes accurate and complete'],['Presiding officer','Natural confidence with terms']])]],
['A study and practice plan',[
'**Fall:** everyone reads RONR In Brief and starts flashcards on the table of rules. **Winter:** take timed practice tests; assign roles; write the previous meeting’s minutes, treasurer’s report and committee reports. **Spring:** practice mock meetings with randomly drawn motions under real timing—15 minutes to prepare and 10 to present.',
'Rotate the motions you practice so the team can handle any combination of subsidiary and privileged motions.',
ex('Weekly practice session','Plan a 60-minute practice (one possible plan).',['10 minutes: flashcard quiz on motion rules','5 minutes: draw four random motions','15 minutes: timed preparation','10 minutes: timed mock meeting','20 minutes: feedback using the rubric'],'Practice mirrors the real Round Two timing.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Preparation room',{a:{text:'A teammate wants to bring Robert’s Rules into the meeting room to check a rule. (Scenario invented for practice.) Is that allowed?',choices:[['No—RONR may be used only during preparation','b','Correct.',true],['Yes, any book is fine','b','Only listed materials are allowed in the meeting.',false]]},b:{text:'After the event, a friend from another chapter asks about your secret topic before their round. What do you do?',choices:[['Decline—the topic is confidential until the event ends','c','Correct.',true],['Share a few hints','c','Revealing it violates professional ethics.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team follows the rules and keeps the topic confidential.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Teams of 5–8 (secondary and postsecondary) take a 50-item, 60-minute test (team average is added to the meeting score), then get a secret topic with one main and four subsidiary or privileged motions, 15 minutes to prepare and 10 minutes to present.','Bring previous minutes, a treasurer’s report and committee reports; RONR may be used only during preparation; the secret topic is confidential; the test is 100% NAP content from RONR In Brief topics.','The 162-point rubric covers order of business (3 each), motions (up to 56), general procedure (10 each), delivery (voice, presence, diction 5 each; participation and discussion 10 each) and minutes (10); every member must take an active role.'],terms:[['Secret topic','A confidential agenda with the motions a team must demonstrate.'],['Presiding officer','The person who chairs the meeting, usually the president.'],['Order of business','The standard sequence of items in a meeting.']],mcqs:[q('How many members may be on a Parliamentary Procedure team?','5–8','2–4','Exactly 4','10–12','Team size.'),q('How long is the meeting presentation?','10 minutes','5 minutes','15 minutes','20 minutes','New for 2026–27.'),q('How long is preparation time?','15 minutes','5 minutes','30 minutes','1 hour','Planning.'),q('How many required motions besides the main motion are on the secret topic?','Four','Two','Six','One','Secret topic.'),q('When may teams use Robert’s Rules?','During preparation only','During the meeting','Never','During the test','Rule.'),q('How is the test used?','The team average is added to the meeting score','It is only for seeding','It is not used','Only the highest score counts','Final scoring.'),q('How many points is the meeting rubric worth?','162','100','56','130','Total.'),q('Must the minutes be read aloud in the meeting?','No; they may be noted as previously distributed','Yes, every word','Only by judges','They are not used','Rubric note.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how the Parliamentary Procedure event works and how your team would organize roles and practice.',rubric:[['Event',['5-8','50','60 minutes','average','secret topic','15 minutes','10 minutes']],['Materials and rules',['minutes','treasurer','committee','ronr','preparation','confidential']],['Roles',['president','secretary','treasurer','committee chair','members','active']],['Scoring and practice',['order of business','motions','presiding officer','delivery','minutes','mock meetings']]],model:'Parliamentary Procedure is a team event for five to eight secondary or postsecondary members. In Round One, each member takes a 50-item, 60-minute multiple-choice test based entirely on National Association of Parliamentarians content from Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised In Brief, and the team’s average both qualifies us for Round Two and is added to our meeting score. In Round Two, we receive a confidential secret topic with one main motion and four subsidiary or privileged motions, have 15 minutes to plan—when we may use RONR—and then conduct a complete 10-minute business meeting, gavel to gavel, without the book. We bring minutes of a previous chapter meeting, a treasurer’s report and committee reports, along with our secret topic copies, blank paper and the president’s pencil. Our roles would include a confident president to preside, a secretary for the minutes, a treasurer, two committee chairs and members who make motions, second and debate, so every member takes an active role. Judges score the 162-point rubric: proper order of business from call to order to adjournment, correct demonstration of the main and required motions, recognition, parliamentary terms, the agenda and the presiding officer’s skill, delivery including team participation and quality of discussion, and our previous minutes. We would study RONR In Brief all year with flashcards and timed practice tests, then run weekly mock meetings with randomly drawn motions under real 15- and 10-minute timing, using the rubric for feedback.'},sources:[RULES,NAP]};

export const PP2={title:'Why rules and how meetings work',hook:'Why should a HOSA chapter follow formal rules just to decide where to hold a fundraiser? Because rules protect everyone’s right to be heard, help the majority decide and keep meetings fair and efficient. This lesson covers the purpose of parliamentary procedure and the structure of a business meeting—the foundation for every motion that follows.',objectives:['Explain the purposes and principles of parliamentary procedure.','Define quorum and describe the roles of the chair and members.','Describe the standard order of business.','Use correct language to obtain the floor and conduct business.'],sections:[
['Why have rules',[
'**Parliamentary procedure** gives groups a common set of rules for making decisions. Its principles include **majority rule**, **protection of the rights of the minority** and of absent members, **one question at a time**, **full and free discussion**, **equal rights and responsibilities** for all members and **fairness and good faith**.',
'Organizations adopt a **parliamentary authority**—usually **Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised (RONR)**—in their bylaws. The organization’s own rules (such as bylaws) take precedence over RONR where they conflict.',
'Knowing why rules exist helps you apply them sensibly in unfamiliar situations.',
"Rules also make meetings efficient. Without an agreed process, groups can spend a long time arguing about how to decide rather than what to decide.",
flip('Principles',[['Majority rule','The majority decides'],['Minority rights','Everyone may speak and be heard'],['One question at a time','Only one main motion pending'],['Full discussion','Debate before deciding'],['Equality','All members have equal rights'],['Parliamentary authority','RONR, adopted in bylaws']])]],
['Quorum and meetings',[
'A **quorum** is the minimum number of members who must be present for business to be validly transacted. Unless the bylaws specify otherwise, it is a **majority of the members**. Without a quorum, the group can only take limited actions such as fixing a time to adjourn, adjourning, recessing or taking measures to obtain a quorum.',
'A **meeting** is a single gathering; a **session** may include one or more meetings on the same order of business. **Regular meetings** are held at times set by the rules; **special meetings** are called for specific business with proper notice.',
'The chair should confirm a quorum before conducting business.',
"If a quorum is lost during a meeting, the chair should not continue normal business. Members may note the absence of a quorum, and any business done without one is not valid unless later ratified.",
chk('Unless the bylaws say otherwise, what is a quorum?',['A majority of the members','All members','Ten members','One-third of the guests'],'Default rule.')]],
['The chair and members',[
'The **chair** (presiding officer) opens the meeting, announces business, recognizes members, states and puts questions to a vote, announces results, enforces rules and maintains order—while remaining **impartial**. The chair refers to themself as “**the chair**,” not “I.” The president is addressed as **“Mr. President” or “Madam President.”** If someone without a title presides, “Mr. Chairman” or “Madam Chairman” is appropriate.',
'**Members** have the right to attend, make motions, debate and vote. To speak, a member must **obtain the floor**: rise (or raise a hand in small groups), address the chair and wait to be **recognized**—“The chair recognizes Member Lopez.”',
'Proper recognition of the chair and members is a 10-point rubric item.',
"A member who has been recognized has the floor and should not be interrupted except by motions that allow interruption, such as a point of order or a question of privilege.",
order('Obtaining the floor',['Member rises when no one has the floor','Member addresses the chair: “Madam President”','Chair recognizes the member by name or role','Member speaks or makes a motion','Member yields the floor when finished'])]],
['The order of business',[
'A standard **order of business** for an organization that meets regularly is: **call to order**; **reading and approval of minutes**; **reports of officers** (including the treasurer); **reports of standing committees**; **reports of special committees**; **special orders**; **unfinished business and general orders**; **new business**; and **adjournment**. Announcements may come near the end.',
'The rubric awards 3 points each for call to order, approval of minutes, treasurer’s report, committee report(s), unfinished business, new business (secret topic) and adjournment—21 easy points if the team follows the agenda.',
"The agenda for a particular meeting may list specific items under each heading. A secret topic works like an agenda: it tells you what business must be presented under new business.",
order('Standard order of business',['Call to order','Approval of minutes','Reports of officers (treasurer)','Reports of committees','Unfinished business','New business','Adjournment'])]],
['Key meeting language',[
'The chair uses set phrases: “**The meeting will come to order**” (with a gavel tap); “**The secretary will read the minutes**” or “**The minutes were distributed. Are there any corrections?… There being none, the minutes are approved as distributed**”; “**Is there any unfinished business?**”; “**Is there any new business?**”; and “**If there is no further business, the meeting is adjourned**” (with a gavel tap).',
'Members say “**I move that…**,” “**Second**” and “**Point of order!**” Using these phrases correctly earns points for proper parliamentary terms.',
"Members can also use formal language to ask questions without making motions, such as “I rise to a parliamentary inquiry” or “I rise for a point of information.”",
match('Situation and correct phrase',[['Opening the meeting','“The meeting will come to order.”'],['Approving minutes','“There being no corrections, the minutes are approved as distributed.”'],['Making a motion','“I move that…”'],['Supporting a motion','“Second.”'],['Taking up new business','“Is there any new business?”'],['Ending the meeting','“The meeting is adjourned.”']])]],
['General consent',[
'Routine or uncontested matters can be decided by **general consent** (unanimous consent) instead of a formal vote. The chair says, “**If there is no objection…**” and pauses; if no one objects, the action is taken. If any member objects, the chair takes a regular vote.',
'General consent is commonly used to approve minutes and to handle simple procedural matters, saving time in a 10-minute meeting.',
ex('Using general consent','Approve the minutes by general consent.',['Chair: “The minutes were distributed in advance. Are there any corrections?”','Chair pauses for corrections','Chair: “There being no corrections, the minutes stand approved as distributed.”'],'The minutes are approved without a formal vote, saving time.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Opening the meeting',{a:{text:'The president opens with, “Okay, I guess we can start now.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the correct language?',choices:[['Tap the gavel and say, “The meeting will come to order.”','b','Correct.',true],['“Hey everyone, let’s begin.”','b','Use formal language.',false]]},b:{text:'A member starts speaking without being recognized. What should the chair do?',choices:[['Politely remind the member to obtain the floor and then recognize them','c','Correct.',true],['Let several members talk at once','c','Members must be recognized.',false]]},c:{text:'The meeting proceeds in proper order.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Parliamentary procedure protects majority rule, minority and absent members’ rights, one question at a time, full discussion and equality; RONR is the usual parliamentary authority, but bylaws prevail.','A quorum is a majority of members unless bylaws say otherwise; the chair is impartial, refers to itself as “the chair” and recognizes members, who must obtain the floor before speaking.','Order of business: call to order, minutes, officers’ reports, committee reports, unfinished business, new business, adjournment; use formal phrases and general consent for routine matters.'],terms:[['Parliamentary authority','The rule book an organization adopts, usually RONR.'],['Obtaining the floor','Being recognized by the chair before speaking.'],['General consent','Approving an action when no member objects.']],mcqs:[q('Unless the bylaws say otherwise, a quorum is:','A majority of the members','All members','Two members','Any number present','Default.'),q('How does the chair refer to themself?','“The chair”','“I”','“Me”','“The boss”','Impartial.'),q('How should a member address the president?','“Madam President” or “Mr. President”','“Hey you”','By first name','“Chair person over there”','Recognition.'),q('Which comes first in the order of business?','Call to order','New business','Adjournment','Committee reports','Sequence.'),q('Which phrase opens a meeting?','“The meeting will come to order.”','“Let’s go.”','“Start now.”','“Hello, team.”','Formal language.'),q('General consent means:','Action is taken if no one objects','A two-thirds vote','A secret ballot','Adjourning immediately','Unanimous consent.'),q('Which has precedence if bylaws conflict with RONR?','The bylaws','RONR','The chair’s opinion','The newest member','Hierarchy.'),q('Unfinished business comes:','Before new business','After adjournment','Before the call to order','Instead of minutes','Order.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain why organizations use parliamentary procedure and describe how a business meeting is conducted from call to order to adjournment.',rubric:[['Purpose',['majority','minority','one question','discussion','equal','fair']],['Quorum and roles',['quorum','majority of members','chair','impartial','recognize','floor']],['Order of business',['call to order','minutes','treasurer','committee','unfinished','new business','adjournment']],['Language',['come to order','i move','second','general consent','no objection','adjourned']]],model:'Organizations use parliamentary procedure so that decisions are made fairly and efficiently: the majority decides while the rights of the minority and absent members are protected, only one question is considered at a time, members discuss fully before deciding and everyone has equal rights. Most groups adopt Robert’s Rules of Order Newly Revised as their parliamentary authority in their bylaws, and the bylaws take precedence when they conflict. Before conducting business, the chair confirms a quorum, which is a majority of the members unless the bylaws say otherwise. The chair, addressed as “Madam President” or “Mr. President,” remains impartial, refers to themself as “the chair” and recognizes members, who must obtain the floor by rising, addressing the chair and being recognized before speaking. A regular meeting follows a standard order of business. The president taps the gavel and says, “The meeting will come to order.” Next, the minutes are approved—often by general consent: “The minutes were distributed. Are there any corrections? There being none, the minutes stand approved as distributed.” Then the treasurer gives the treasurer’s report and committee chairs give their reports, followed by unfinished business and new business, in which members say “I move that…” and others “Second.” Finally, when there is no further business, the chair announces, “The meeting is adjourned,” with a tap of the gavel. Following this order and using these formal phrases earns many points in Round Two.'},sources:[RULES,RONR]};

export const PP3={title:'Handling motions',hook:'A motion is how a member brings business before the group, and nearly half of the Round Two rubric depends on demonstrating motions correctly. Every motion follows the same basic steps, but each type has its own rules about seconds, debate, amendment and vote. Learning the steps and the order of precedence is the key to running a smooth meeting.',objectives:['Describe the six steps for handling a motion.','Classify motions as main, subsidiary, privileged, incidental or bring-back.','Apply the order of precedence of motions.','Use the table of rules to recall each motion’s characteristics.'],sections:[
['Six steps of a motion',[
'Every motion is handled in six steps. **(1)** A member obtains the floor and **makes the motion**: “I move that…” **(2)** Another member **seconds** it (a second shows at least two members want to discuss it; no recognition is needed to second). **(3)** The chair **states the question**: “It is moved and seconded that… Is there any debate?” **(4)** Members **debate**. **(5)** The chair **puts the question** to a vote: “Those in favor, say aye. Those opposed, say no.” **(6)** The chair **announces the result**: “The ayes have it, and the motion is adopted,” and states what happens next.',
'Once the chair states the question, the motion belongs to the assembly, not the maker.',
'A complete announcement includes the vote result, whether the motion is adopted or lost and the effect.',
order('Six steps of a motion',['Member makes the motion','Another member seconds','Chair states the question','Members debate','Chair puts the question to a vote','Chair announces the result and its effect'])]],
['Main motions',[
'A **main motion** brings new business before the assembly. It can be made only when no other business is pending. It **requires a second**, is **debatable**, is **amendable** and usually needs a **majority vote**.',
'Motions should be worded clearly and in the positive: “I move that our chapter hold a blood drive on March 12.” Avoid motions phrased in the negative, which confuse voters.',
'The main motion is the first item in the rubric’s motions section.',
"If a main motion is complex, the chair or a member may ask that it be put in writing so its exact wording is clear before debate and amendment.",
chk('When can a main motion be made?',['When no other business is pending','While another main motion is being debated','Only after adjournment','Only during the treasurer’s report'],'One question at a time.')]],
['Classes of motions',[
'**Subsidiary motions** help the assembly deal with a pending main motion: postpone indefinitely, amend, commit or refer, postpone to a certain time, limit or extend limits of debate, previous question and lay on the table. **Privileged motions** concern urgent matters unrelated to the pending business: call for the orders of the day, raise a question of privilege, recess, adjourn and fix the time to which to adjourn. **Incidental motions** deal with procedure: point of order, appeal, suspend the rules, objection to consideration, division of the assembly, division of a question, parliamentary inquiry, request for information and withdraw a motion. **Motions that bring a question again before the assembly** include take from the table, reconsider and rescind or amend something previously adopted.',
'Secret topics feature **subsidiary and privileged** motions, so master those first.',
sort('Class of motion',['Subsidiary','Privileged','Incidental'],[['Commit or refer',0],['Recess',1],['Point of order',2],['Postpone to a certain time',0],['Question of privilege',1],['Division of the assembly',2],['Lay on the table',0],['Fix the time to which to adjourn',1]])]],
['The order of precedence',[
'Motions have a **rank**. When a motion is pending, any higher-ranking motion may be made, but not a lower-ranking one. From **highest to lowest**: fix the time to which to adjourn; adjourn; recess; raise a question of privilege; call for the orders of the day; lay on the table; previous question; limit or extend limits of debate; postpone to a certain time; commit or refer; amend; postpone indefinitely; and the main motion.',
'Pending motions are voted on in **reverse order**—the most recently made (highest-ranking) first. Incidental motions have no fixed rank; they arise as needed.',
order('Order of precedence (highest to lowest)',['Fix the time to which to adjourn','Adjourn','Recess','Raise a question of privilege','Call for the orders of the day','Lay on the table','Previous question','Limit or extend limits of debate','Postpone to a certain time','Commit or refer','Amend','Postpone indefinitely','Main motion'])]],
['The table of rules',[
'For each motion, know: Does it need a **second**? Is it **debatable**? Is it **amendable**? What **vote** does it need? Can it **interrupt** a speaker? Examples: **postpone indefinitely**—second, debatable (opens the main question to debate), not amendable, majority. **Commit**—second, debatable, amendable, majority. **Postpone to a certain time**—second, debatable, amendable, majority. **Limit or extend debate** and **previous question**—second, not debatable, two-thirds (previous question is not amendable). **Lay on the table**—second, not debatable, not amendable, majority. **Recess**—second, not debatable, amendable, majority. **Adjourn**—second, not debatable, not amendable, majority.',
'Two-thirds votes protect members’ rights when debate is limited or closed.',
{t:'table',caption:'Table of rules for common subsidiary and privileged motions (RONR)',head:['Motion','Second','Debatable','Amendable','Vote'],rows:[['Postpone indefinitely','Yes','Yes','No','Majority'],['Amend','Yes','If motion is','Yes','Majority'],['Commit or refer','Yes','Yes','Yes','Majority'],['Postpone to a certain time','Yes','Yes','Yes','Majority'],['Limit or extend debate','Yes','No','Yes','Two-thirds'],['Previous question','Yes','No','No','Two-thirds'],['Lay on the table','Yes','No','No','Majority'],['Recess','Yes','No','Yes','Majority'],['Adjourn','Yes','No','No','Majority']]}]],
['Withdrawing and modifying motions',[
'Before the chair states the question, the maker may **withdraw or modify** the motion freely. After the motion is stated, it belongs to the assembly, and the maker must request permission to withdraw it; this is usually granted by general consent.',
'The chair may help members word motions clearly but should not change their meaning.',
ex('Processing a main motion','Script the opening of a main motion.',['Member: “Madam President.” Chair: “The chair recognizes Member Kim.”','Member: “I move that our chapter donate $200 to the local food bank.”','Another member: “Second.”','Chair: “It is moved and seconded that our chapter donate $200 to the local food bank. Is there any debate?”'],'The motion is properly made, seconded and stated, ready for debate.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Precedence drill',{a:{text:'A main motion and an amendment are pending. A member moves to refer the motion to a committee. (Scenario invented for practice.) Is it in order?',choices:[['Yes—commit or refer ranks above amend','b','Correct.',true],['No—nothing can be made while an amendment is pending','b','Higher-ranking motions are in order.',false]]},b:{text:'With all three pending, which is voted on first?',choices:[['The motion to refer','c','Correct—reverse order of making.',true],['The main motion','c','Vote on the most recent, highest-ranking motion first.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team adds this sequence to its practice script.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Six steps: make (“I move that…”), second, chair states the question, debate, chair puts the question and chair announces the result and effect; after stating, the motion belongs to the assembly.','Main motions need a second, are debatable and amendable, need a majority and can be made only when nothing is pending; motions are subsidiary, privileged, incidental or bring-back.','Order of precedence (high to low): fix time to adjourn, adjourn, recess, question of privilege, orders of the day, lay on the table, previous question, limit/extend debate, postpone to a certain time, commit, amend, postpone indefinitely, main motion; learn each motion’s second, debate, amendment and vote.'],terms:[['Main motion','A motion that brings new business before the assembly.'],['Subsidiary motion','A motion that helps process a pending main motion.'],['Order of precedence','The ranking that determines which motions may be made while others are pending.']],mcqs:[q('What is the first step in handling a motion?','A member makes the motion','The chair announces the result','Members vote','Debate','Six steps.'),q('After which step does a motion belong to the assembly?','The chair states the question','The motion is made','The vote is taken','It never does','Ownership.'),q('Which motion ranks highest?','Fix the time to which to adjourn','Adjourn','Recess','Lay on the table','Precedence.'),q('Which ranks lowest among subsidiary motions?','Postpone indefinitely','Amend','Commit','Lay on the table','Precedence.'),q('Which vote does the previous question require?','Two-thirds','Majority','Unanimous','None','Closes debate.'),q('Is lay on the table debatable?','No','Yes','Only by the chair','Only once','Table of rules.'),q('Which is a privileged motion?','Recess','Commit','Amend','Point of order','Class.'),q('Pending motions are voted on in:','Reverse order of making','Alphabetical order','The order they were made','Random order','Most recent first.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a motion is handled and how the order of precedence works, using examples of subsidiary and privileged motions.',rubric:[['Six steps',['make','second','states the question','debate','puts the question','announces']],['Main motion rules',['second','debatable','amendable','majority','no other business']],['Classes and precedence',['subsidiary','privileged','incidental','precedence','higher','reverse order']],['Table of rules',['previous question','two-thirds','lay on the table','not debatable','commit','recess','adjourn']]],model:'Every motion follows six steps. A member obtains the floor and says, “I move that our chapter donate $200 to the food bank”; another member seconds; the chair states the question—“It is moved and seconded that… Is there any debate?”—and from that point the motion belongs to the assembly; members debate; the chair puts the question—“Those in favor, say aye. Those opposed, say no”; and the chair announces the result and its effect: “The ayes have it, and the motion is adopted. The treasurer will send the donation.” A main motion requires a second, is debatable and amendable, needs a majority and can be made only when no other business is pending. Subsidiary motions help process the main motion, privileged motions handle urgent matters and incidental motions deal with procedure. Motions follow an order of precedence from fix the time to which to adjourn, adjourn, recess, question of privilege and orders of the day down through lay on the table, previous question, limit or extend debate, postpone to a certain time, commit, amend, postpone indefinitely and the main motion. While a motion is pending, only higher-ranking motions may be made, and pending motions are voted on in reverse order. For example, if a main motion and an amendment are pending, a member may move to refer the motion to a committee, which would be voted on first. I would also know each motion’s rules: the previous question and limiting debate need two-thirds and are not debatable, lay on the table is not debatable and needs a majority, commit is debatable and amendable, and recess and adjourn are not debatable.'},sources:[RULES,RONR]};

export const PP4={title:'Debate and limiting debate',hook:'Debate is where members persuade one another—and where Round Two’s quality of discussion score is earned. RONR sets clear rules for who speaks, how often and how long, and it provides motions to limit or close debate when needed. A team that debates properly shows real diversity of viewpoints while keeping the meeting moving.',objectives:['Apply RONR’s basic rules of debate.','Maintain decorum in debate.','Use motions to limit, extend or close debate.','Plan quality discussion for a 10-minute meeting.'],sections:[
['Basic rules of debate',[
'After the chair states a debatable question, members may **debate** it. The **maker of the motion** has the right to speak first. Under RONR, unless the organization’s rules say otherwise, each member may speak **twice on the same question on the same day**, for up to **10 minutes** each time, but a member may not speak a second time while anyone who has not yet spoken wishes to speak.',
'The chair tries to **alternate** between speakers for and against the motion. Debate must be **germane**—related to the pending question.',
'In a 10-minute competition meeting, speeches should be short—one or two sentences each.',
"Members may not speak against their own motion, although they may vote against it. A member who changes their mind can explain this in debate before the vote.",
flip('Debate rules',[['First speaker','The maker of the motion'],['Number of speeches','Two per member per question per day'],['Length','Up to 10 minutes each (RONR default)'],['Second speech','Only after all who wish have spoken once'],['Alternation','Chair alternates for and against'],['Germane','Debate must relate to the pending question']])]],
['Decorum',[
'Members address all remarks **through the chair**, not to each other. They speak about **measures, not motives**—criticizing an idea, never a person. Members avoid using other members’ names when possible (“the previous speaker”), and they do not interrupt except for motions that allow interruption, such as a point of order.',
'The chair should remain **impartial** and not take part in debate while presiding. If the chair wishes to debate, they must give up the chair until the question is decided.',
'Courteous debate also improves stage presence and quality of discussion scores.',
"The chair may interrupt a speaker who is out of order, such as one who is speaking off topic or making personal attacks, and remind them to keep remarks germane and courteous.",
sort('Proper decorum?',['Proper','Improper'],[['“Through the chair, I believe the cost is too high.”',0],['“Jordan only wants this to look good.”',1],['Referring to “the previous speaker”',0],['Interrupting a speaker to disagree',1],['Speaking to the motion, not the person',0]])]],
['Limiting or extending debate',[
'The motion to **limit or extend limits of debate** changes the number or length of speeches—for example, “I move that debate be limited to one minute per speaker,” or “I move to extend debate by five minutes.” It requires a **second**, is **not debatable**, is **amendable** and requires a **two-thirds vote**.',
'Two-thirds is required because limiting debate restricts members’ rights. Extending limits also needs two-thirds because it changes the rules for the question.',
'This motion is common on secret topics.',
"Limits adopted through this motion usually apply only to the pending question and the current meeting, unless the motion specifies otherwise. They end when the question is decided.",
chk('What vote does a motion to limit debate require?',['Two-thirds','Majority','Unanimous','No vote'],'Restricts rights, so two-thirds.')]],
['The previous question',[
'The **previous question** (“I move the previous question” or “I move to close debate”) asks the assembly to **stop debate and amendment immediately** and vote. It requires a **second**, is **not debatable**, is **not amendable** and requires a **two-thirds vote**. If adopted, the chair immediately puts the pending question(s) to a vote.',
'Members should not shout “Question!” to force a vote—only the adopted motion for the previous question closes debate. The chair may ask, “Is there any further debate?” and, hearing none, put the question.',
'A previous question can apply to only the immediately pending question or to a series of pending questions, as the motion specifies.',
"When the previous question is adopted on a main motion with an amendment pending, it may apply to the amendment only or to both, depending on how it is worded—“I move the previous question on all pending questions” closes debate on everything.",
order('Processing the previous question',['Member: “I move the previous question.”','Another member seconds','Chair states it and takes a vote (no debate)','Chair announces whether two-thirds voted in favor','If adopted, the chair immediately puts the main question to a vote'])]],
['Voting on a two-thirds motion',[
'Two-thirds votes should usually be taken by a **rising vote** (or show of hands in small groups) so the chair can see the count: “Those in favor will rise… Be seated. Those opposed will rise… Be seated.” Two-thirds means at least **twice as many yes votes as no votes** among those voting.',
'Announce results clearly: “There are two-thirds in the affirmative, and the motion for the previous question is adopted. We will now vote on the main motion.”',
"Agree in advance on which members will argue each side. Assigning viewpoints before the meeting ensures debate sounds genuine and that no one is caught without something to say.",
ex('Is it two-thirds?','A vote on limiting debate is 8 in favor, 4 opposed (invented for practice). Does it pass?',['Two-thirds requires yes votes to be at least twice the no votes','2 × 4 = 8','8 yes votes equals 8','8 is at least 8'],'Yes—8 to 4 is exactly two-thirds, so it passes (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Quality discussion in a 10-minute meeting',[
'The rubric’s **quality of discussion** item rewards accomplishing the secret topic’s goals with **quality discussion and diversity of viewpoints**. Plan brief arguments for and against each debatable motion—cost, timing, member interest, benefit to the community—so the debate sounds real.',
'Assign speakers in advance and keep each speech to about 10–20 seconds. Use realistic chapter details that match your previous minutes and reports.',
match('Viewpoint and sample debate point',[['For the blood drive','“It supports our community service goal.”'],['Against the date','“March conflicts with state conference.”'],['For referring','“A committee can compare venues.”'],['Against limiting debate','“Members still have questions.”'],['For postponing','“We need the treasurer’s budget first.”'],['For adopting','“We have enough volunteers.”']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Debate drill',{a:{text:'During debate, a member shouts, “Question! Question!” (Scenario invented for practice.) What should the chair do?',choices:[['Explain that debate continues unless the previous question is moved and adopted by two-thirds','b','Correct.',true],['Immediately take the vote','b','Shouting does not close debate.',false]]},b:{text:'A member says, “Sam is wrong because he never helps.” How should the chair respond?',choices:[['Remind members to speak about the measure, not motives or personalities','c','Correct.',true],['Ignore it','c','The chair maintains decorum.',false]]},c:{text:'Debate continues courteously and on topic.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The maker speaks first; under RONR each member may speak twice per question per day (up to 10 minutes each), second speeches only after others; the chair alternates sides; debate must be germane.','Decorum: speak through the chair, about measures not motives, avoid names and interruptions; the chair stays impartial.','Limit/extend debate (second, not debatable, amendable, two-thirds) and the previous question (second, not debatable, not amendable, two-thirds) close or restrict debate; use rising votes; plan short, diverse debate points for quality discussion.'],terms:[['Germane','Closely related to the pending question.'],['Previous question','A motion to close debate and vote immediately, requiring two-thirds.'],['Decorum','Proper, courteous conduct in debate.']],mcqs:[q('Who has the right to speak first in debate?','The maker of the motion','The chair','The secretary','The newest member','Rule.'),q('Under RONR, how many times may a member speak on the same question on the same day?','Twice','Once','Three times','Unlimited','Default.'),q('How long may each speech be under RONR’s default rule?','10 minutes','1 minute','5 minutes','30 minutes','Default.'),q('What vote does the previous question require?','Two-thirds','Majority','Unanimous','None','Rule.'),q('Is the motion to limit debate debatable?','No','Yes','Only once','Only by the maker','Rule.'),q('Members should address remarks:','Through the chair','Directly to opponents','To the audience','To the judges','Decorum.'),q('Does shouting “Question!” close debate?','No','Yes','Only twice','Only for the chair','Proper method.'),q('A vote of 10 in favor and 6 opposed on the previous question:','Fails (needs at least 12 yes)','Passes','Is a tie','Needs no vote','2 × 6 = 12.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the rules of debate and how a team would limit or close debate during a meeting.',rubric:[['Debate rules',['maker','first','twice','10 minutes','alternate','germane']],['Decorum',['through the chair','measures','motives','impartial','interrupt']],['Limiting and closing',['limit','extend','previous question','two-thirds','not debatable','second']],['Voting and discussion',['rising vote','twice as many','quality','viewpoints','short']]],model:'After the chair states a debatable question, the maker of the motion has the right to speak first. Under RONR’s default rules, each member may speak twice on the same question on the same day, up to 10 minutes each, but no one may speak a second time while someone who has not spoken wishes to, and the chair tries to alternate between speakers for and against. All debate must be germane to the pending question, and members address their remarks through the chair, speaking about measures rather than motives and avoiding personal attacks, while the chair remains impartial and does not debate while presiding. To limit or extend debate, a member might say, “I move that debate be limited to one minute per speaker”; this motion needs a second, is not debatable, is amendable and requires a two-thirds vote because it restricts members’ rights. To close debate, a member says, “I move the previous question,” which needs a second, is not debatable or amendable and requires two-thirds; if adopted, the chair immediately puts the pending question to a vote. Shouting “Question!” does not close debate. Two-thirds votes should be taken by rising vote or show of hands, and pass when yes votes are at least twice the no votes. In our 10-minute meeting, we would plan short arguments for and against each debatable motion, assign speakers and use realistic chapter details to show quality discussion and diverse viewpoints.'},sources:[RULES,RONR,NAPU]};

export const PP5={title:'Amendments',hook:'Amendments let a group improve a motion before voting on it—changing a date, adding a budget limit or rewording a confusing phrase. They are among the most common motions in real meetings and on secret topics. Knowing the forms of amendment and the two-level limit lets your team modify motions smoothly and correctly.',objectives:['Describe the purpose and forms of amendments.','Distinguish primary and secondary amendments.','Apply the germaneness rule and amendment limits.','Process amendments in the correct order.'],sections:[
['Purpose of amending',[
'The motion to **amend** modifies the wording of a pending motion before it is voted on. It is a **subsidiary motion** that requires a **second**, is **debatable** if the motion being amended is debatable, is itself amendable (once) and requires a **majority vote**—even if the motion being amended needs two-thirds.',
'Amending is used to clarify, improve or adjust a motion so it can gain wider support.',
'A member who wants a completely different action should usually oppose the motion and make a new main motion later.',
"Before amending, consider whether the change could be accomplished more simply by voting the motion down and making a new one. Amendments work best for specific improvements, not complete reversals.",
chk('What vote does a primary amendment usually require?',['A majority','Two-thirds','Unanimous','None'],'Even when the main motion needs two-thirds.')]],
['Forms of amendment',[
'There are three basic forms. **Insert** (or **add** at the end) words: “I move to amend by adding ‘on March 12.’” **Strike out** words: “I move to amend by striking out ‘and refreshments.’” **Strike out and insert** words: “I move to amend by striking out ‘$200’ and inserting ‘$150.’”',
'A **substitute** is a form of strike out and insert that replaces an entire paragraph, section or the whole motion. Amendments should be stated precisely so everyone knows exactly what changes.',
'The chair restates the motion as it would read if the amendment is adopted.',
"When stating an amendment, the chair should read the words to be changed, the change and how the motion would read if the amendment is adopted, so every member knows exactly what they are voting on.",
match('Amendment form and example',[['Insert','Insert “annual” before “blood drive”'],['Add','Add “on March 12” at the end'],['Strike out','Strike out “and refreshments”'],['Strike out and insert','Strike out “$200” and insert “$150”'],['Substitute','Replace the whole motion with a new version'],['Restating','Chair reads the motion as it would be amended']])]],
['Primary and secondary amendments',[
'A **primary amendment** (first degree) amends the main motion. A **secondary amendment** (second degree) amends the primary amendment. **No third-degree amendments** are allowed—you cannot amend a secondary amendment. However, after a secondary amendment is decided, another secondary amendment may be offered, and after a primary amendment is decided, another primary amendment may be offered.',
'Only one primary and one secondary amendment may be **pending** at the same time.',
'The secondary amendment must relate to the primary amendment, not to other parts of the main motion.',
"A practical way to remember degree limits is “two levels at a time.” Once the secondary amendment is voted on, the floor is open for another secondary amendment to the same primary amendment.",
sort('Primary or secondary amendment?',['Primary (amends the main motion)','Secondary (amends the amendment)'],[['Main motion: donate $200. Amendment: strike “$200,” insert “$150.”',0],['Pending amendment “$150.” Amendment: strike “$150,” insert “$175.”',1],['Main motion: hold a blood drive. Amendment: add “in March.”',0],['Pending amendment “in March.” Amendment: insert “early” before “March.”',1]])]],
['Germaneness and improper amendments',[
'An amendment must be **germane**—closely related to the subject of the motion it would amend. An amendment to a motion about a blood drive that adds “and buy new jackets” is not germane. The chair rules non-germane amendments **out of order**.',
'An amendment is also improper if it merely turns an affirmative into a negative (changing “adopt” to “not adopt”), since voting no achieves that. Amendments may be hostile to the original intent but must still be germane.',
"Germaneness applies to secondary amendments too: a secondary amendment must relate to the primary amendment, not to other parts of the main motion.",
sort('Germane or not germane?',['Germane','Not germane'],[['Changing the blood drive date',0],['Adding a fundraiser for jackets to a blood drive motion',1],['Limiting the donation to $150',0],['Adding “not” to reverse the motion',1],['Specifying the blood drive location',0]])]],
['Processing amendments in order',[
'When a main motion, a primary amendment and a secondary amendment are pending, debate and vote proceed from the **most recent** back: the secondary amendment first, then the primary amendment (as amended, if the secondary passed), then the main motion (as amended, if the primary passed).',
'After each vote, the chair announces the result and states the next pending question: “The secondary amendment is adopted. The question is now on the primary amendment, as amended, to strike out ‘$200’ and insert ‘$175.’”',
"Keep notes during the meeting so the chair can track exactly what is pending. Writing each motion and amendment on paper prevents confusion when several are pending at once.",
ex('Processing amendments','Order the votes (example).',['Main motion: donate $200 to the food bank','Primary amendment: strike “$200,” insert “$150”','Secondary amendment: strike “$150,” insert “$175” → vote first (adopted)','Primary amendment as amended (“$175”) → vote second (adopted)','Main motion as amended: donate $175 → vote last'],'Vote secondary, then primary, then main motion as amended.')]],
['Friendly amendments',[
'A so-called “**friendly amendment**” is one the maker of the motion supports. Under RONR, once the chair has stated the motion, a friendly amendment is **still handled like any amendment**—it needs a second (or general consent) and a vote. The maker cannot simply accept it on behalf of the assembly.',
'The chair can speed things up by asking for **general consent**: “Is there any objection to adopting the amendment?… Hearing none, the amendment is adopted.”',
"In competition, a smooth amendment sequence shows judges strong parliamentary skill. Practice restating the main motion as amended clearly after each vote.",
chk('After a motion is stated, can the maker alone accept a “friendly amendment”?',['No—the assembly must adopt it by vote or general consent','Yes, the maker owns the motion','Only the secretary decides','Only if the judges agree'],'The motion belongs to the assembly.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Amendment drill',{a:{text:'A primary and a secondary amendment are pending. A member moves to amend the secondary amendment. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should the chair rule?',choices:[['Out of order—third-degree amendments are not allowed','b','Correct.',true],['In order','b','Only two levels are permitted.',false]]},b:{text:'A member moves to amend a blood drive motion by adding “and buy new chapter jackets.” What should the chair rule?',choices:[['Out of order—the amendment is not germane','c','Correct.',true],['In order','c','It is unrelated to the blood drive.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team practices restating motions after each amendment vote.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Amend modifies a pending motion; it needs a second, is debatable if the motion is, and needs a majority vote (even if the main motion needs two-thirds).','Forms: insert or add, strike out, strike out and insert, and substitute; primary amendments amend the main motion, secondary amendments amend the primary; no third degree; only one of each pending at a time.','Amendments must be germane and cannot simply negate; vote from secondary to primary to main motion as amended; “friendly amendments” still need a vote or general consent.'],terms:[['Primary amendment','An amendment to the main motion.'],['Secondary amendment','An amendment to a pending primary amendment.'],['Substitute','Replacing an entire paragraph or motion with new text.']],mcqs:[q('What vote does an amendment usually require?','Majority','Two-thirds','Unanimous','None','Rule.'),q('Which is a form of amendment?','Strike out and insert','Adjourn','Recess','Reconsider','Forms.'),q('A secondary amendment amends:','The primary amendment','The main motion directly','The bylaws','The minutes','Degree.'),q('Are third-degree amendments allowed?','No','Yes','Only by the chair','Only on Fridays','Limit.'),q('An amendment must be:','Germane','Hostile','Unanimous','Written in Latin','Relevance.'),q('When a secondary amendment is pending, which is voted on first?','The secondary amendment','The main motion','The primary amendment','None','Reverse order.'),q('Can the maker alone accept a friendly amendment after the motion is stated?','No','Yes','Only twice','Only the treasurer can','Assembly owns it.'),q('A substitute replaces:','An entire paragraph or motion','Only one letter','The chair','The quorum','Form.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how amendments work, including forms, degrees, germaneness and the order of voting, with an example.',rubric:[['Purpose and rules',['amend','second','debatable','majority','subsidiary']],['Forms',['insert','add','strike out','strike out and insert','substitute']],['Degrees and limits',['primary','secondary','third','germane','one pending']],['Order and example',['secondary first','primary','main motion as amended','restate','friendly','general consent']]],model:'The motion to amend changes the wording of a pending motion before the vote. It needs a second, is debatable if the motion it amends is debatable and requires a majority vote, even when the motion being amended needs two-thirds. There are three basic forms: inserting or adding words, striking out words and striking out and inserting words, with a substitute replacing an entire paragraph or motion. A primary amendment amends the main motion, and a secondary amendment amends the primary amendment; third-degree amendments are not allowed, and only one primary and one secondary amendment may be pending at once. Every amendment must be germane to the motion it amends, so the chair would rule out of order an amendment adding “buy new jackets” to a blood drive motion, and an amendment cannot simply turn the motion into its negative. For example, if a member moves that our chapter donate $200 to the food bank, another member might move to amend by striking out “$200” and inserting “$150,” and a third might move a secondary amendment to strike out “$150” and insert “$175.” The chair would process them in reverse order: first the secondary amendment, then the primary amendment as amended, and finally the main motion as amended, restating the question after each vote. If the maker likes an amendment—a so-called friendly amendment—it still must be adopted by a vote or by general consent, because once stated, the motion belongs to the assembly.'},sources:[RULES,RONR]};
